    <name>
      <text>geometric sequence q1</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>A learner in the Science Club is growing bacteria in the school laboratory. The number of bacteria at the end of hours 1, 2, 3 and 4 is:</p>
<p>\({@t1@}, \; {@t2@}, \; {@t3@}, \; {@t4@}, \; \ldots\)</p>
<p>The numbers form a geometric progression (GP). The \(n^{th}\) term of a GP is \( T_n = ar^{n-1} \), where \(a\) is the first term and \(r\) is the common ratio.</p>

<p>(a) Find the common ratio, \(r\).</p>
<p>[[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</p>

<p>(b) Find the number of bacteria at the end of hour 5.</p>
<p>[[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</p>

<p>(c) Write an expression for the \(n^{th}\) term. Use \(n\) in your answer.</p>
<p>[[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prt3]]</p>

<p>(d) At the end of which hour are there {@target@} bacteria?</p>
<p>[[input:ans4]] [[validation:ans4]] [[feedback:prt4]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>The numbers of bacteria are {@t1@}, {@t2@}, {@t3@}, {@t4@}, ... This is a geometric progression (GP), because each term is multiplied by the same number.</p>

<p>(a) <strong>Common ratio:</strong></p>
<p>Divide any term by the term before it:</p>
<p>\[ r = \frac{ {@t2@} }{ {@t1@} } = {@ta1@} \]</p>
<p>Check: \( \frac{ {@t3@} }{ {@t2@} } = {@ta1@} \) and \( \frac{ {@t4@} }{ {@t3@} } = {@ta1@} \). The ratio is always the same.</p>

<p>(b) <strong>Hour 5:</strong></p>
<p>Multiply the 4th term by the common ratio:</p>
<p>\[ T_5 = {@t4@} \times {@r@} = {@ta2@} \]</p>
<p>Check with the rule: \( T_5 = ar^{4} = {@a@} \times {@r@}^{4} = {@a@} \times {@r^4@} = {@ta2@} \).</p>

<p>(c) <strong>The \(n^{th}\) term:</strong></p>
<p>The rule for the \(n^{th}\) term of a GP is:</p>
<p>\[ T_n = ar^{n-1} \]</p>
<p>Here \( a = {@a@} \) and \( r = {@r@} \). Put these into the rule:</p>
<p>\[ T_n = {@a@} \times {@r@}^{n-1} \]</p>
<p>Check with term 1. Put \( n = 1 \). Then \( {@r@}^{0} = 1 \), so \( T_1 = {@a@} \). This is the first term.</p>

<p>(d) <strong>Which hour has {@target@} bacteria?</strong></p>
<p>Set the rule equal to {@target@}:</p>
<p>\[ {@a@} \times {@r@}^{n-1} = {@target@} \]</p>
<p>Divide both sides by {@a@}:</p>
<p>\[ {@r@}^{n-1} = {@target/a@} \]</p>
<p>Write {@target/a@} as a power of {@r@}:</p>
<p>\[ {@r@}^{ {@m-1@} } = {@r^(m-1)@} \]</p>
<p>So \( n - 1 = {@m-1@} \), which gives \( n = {@ta4@} \).</p>
<p>There are {@target@} bacteria at the end of hour {@ta4@}.</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
